# Explicit ACCEPT list for the builtin-name difference between lume-core's
# interpreter registry (src/interp.c) and the lume host (lang/interp.c).
#
# Checked by scripts/check-builtin-parity.py (make check-parity), which runs in
# the CI check-sync job with a sibling lume-core checkout. A name that appears
# in the diff but NOT here is a hard failure: either port the builtin to the
# host, or record the decision below. A name listed here that is no longer in
# the diff is also a failure: the host gained (or core dropped) a builtin, so
# the list must be refreshed instead of quietly rotting.
#
# Why core has 99 builtins the host does not:
#   - trig / hyperbolic (acos..tanh), the statistics and hypothesis-test
#     family (mean..z_test), the distribution CDF/inverse helpers (chisq_p,
#     f_pvalue, norminv, percentile, ...), the 30+ rand_* / sim_* generators
#     and option-pricing helpers (bs_*) are lume-core's language experiment
#     surface. The host is a single server binary and never adopted them.
#   - sql_tx_* come with core's OBJ_SQLTX handle object; the host uses its own
#     app-specific sql_query/sql_write builtins and has no such object kind.
#   - This is also why a core test fixture cannot be copied into the host's
#     semantic baseline blindly: p1-2-consistency.lume calls cos() and fails
#     on the host with "undefined variable 'cos'". Only fixtures made of the
#     shared 127 builtins qualify (see tests/baseline.provenance).
#   - spawn / proc_read / proc_wait / proc_kill are lume-core-only by
#     decision (a), 2026-11-08 (P14). They are gated, allowlisted child
#     processes that live in lume-core's interpreter; its native emitters
#     have no spawn helper (a compiled binary has no VM policy to consult),
#     enforced by lume-core's `make check` -> spawn-native-gate. This host
#     has no spawn gate and no use for a child-process surface, so merging
#     the registration chain would add an UNGATED process primitive.
#     PERMANENT, not a pending port: these names leave this list only if
#     the host first lands an equivalent fail-closed gate, then also
#     updates CORE_ONLY_NAMES in scripts/sync-lang.py, lang/PIN.lag and
#     TODO.md P14. See lume-core/TODO.md P14 for the full decision.
#   - http_stream_open / http_stream_read / http_stream_close are
#     lume-core-only by decision (a), 2026-11-08 (P17). They are an inbound
#     SSE client — the deferred half of P14's streaming work: a generation-
#     tagged slot handle table plus an incremental SSE line parser, reusing
#     the bare-socket HTTP/1.1 transport (url_parse, the host_blocked SSRF
#     gate, open_conn proxy+TLS, read_head) and the shared vm->no_net gate.
#     Like spawn they register only in the interpreter (src/interp.c /
#     typecheck.c / builtins.c / builtins_internal.h / builtins.h) and never
#     reach codegen_call.c or llvm_codegen_internal.h's BUILTINS[], so
#     --compile-text fails with "call to unknown function". rt.c has no
#     stream helper, so a compiled binary simply has no such primitive.
#     This host has no inbound-SSE need and no gate of its own; merging the
#     registration chain would add an UNGATED network primitive to a server
#     binary. PERMANENT, not a pending port: these names leave this list
#     only if the host first lands an equivalent fail-closed gate, then also
#     updates CORE_ONLY_NAMES in scripts/sync-lang.py, lang/PIN.lag and
#     TODO.md P17. See lume-core/TODO.md P17 for the full decision.
#
# host-only names are host additions that core does not carry.
#
# Regenerate after an intentional change:
#   make check-parity        # prints exactly which names to add / remove

[core-only]
acos
acosh
